  ('0d0e3d03-e1fe-588f-a36a-b9878fddd1d4', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'combinatorics-probability'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Three students are chosen at random from a group of $6$ boys and $4$ girls. What is the probability that at least one girl is chosen?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$\\frac{1}{6}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"stopped-at-the-complement"},{"text":"$\\frac{5}{6}$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$\\frac{2}{5}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-a-single-draw"},{"text":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"computed-exactly-one-girl"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$\frac{5}{6}$$q$, $q$Use the complement. There are $_{10}C_3 = 120$ groups, and $_6C_3 = 20$ of them are all boys, so the probability of no girl is $\frac{20}{120} = \frac{1}{6}$. Then the probability of at least one girl is $1 - \frac{1}{6} = \frac{5}{6}$. The value $\frac{1}{6}$ is the all-boys probability, not the answer.$q$, 'draft', null)
